Ladybird recognizes 26 legacy logical spacing, border, and size
properties through its property-alias table. The aliases resolve to
logical longhands, so writing mode, direction, declaration order,
and importance determine their effect through the normal cascade.

Blink lists these names with alias_for in css_properties.json5.
CSSProperties.expand_aliases associates each alias with its logical
property. WebKit lists the same aliases in CSSProperties.json, and
_generate_gperf_keywords maps them to the canonical property IDs.
Ladybird matches both engines for these longhand aliases.

Gecko does not support this set of aliases. Its longhands.toml lists
-moz-margin-start and similar aliases instead, while its counted
unknown-property table includes many of the -webkit- names.
PropertyId::parse_unchecked records their use and rejects them.
Ladybird deliberately accepts these names for compatibility with
pages using the legacy declarations, as requested in the issue.

Chrome confirms CSSOM serialization and access, declaration order,
importance, variables, and logical mapping in horizontal and both
vertical writing modes. The implementation uses Ladybird’s alias
mechanism; no engine code structure or text was copied.

The minimum-size aliases preserve the canonical computed values.
Ladybird reports auto for the blank body used by the default-value
test, while Chrome reports 0px for both the aliases and canonical
properties. The alias registrations leave that difference intact.
